<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naser al-Din Shah Qajar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1831">1831</a>–<a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Naser al-Din Shah was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> shah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> from 1848 to 1896, the longest reign of the dynasty. His minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-kabir">Amir Kabir</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-ul-funun">Dar ol-Fonun</a> college, and the shah travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, but he relied on foreign loans and concessions, provoking the tobacco protest of 1891–1892. He centralised power, ending Kurdish autonomy in Ardalan in 1867, and was assassinated in 1896.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naser-al-din-shah-qajar.jpg" alt="Naser al-Din Shah Qajar" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nadar / Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naser_al-Din_Shah_Qajar,_close_up,_with_slight_smile_by_Nadar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Naser al-Din Mirza was born on 17 July 1831 at Kahnamu, a village near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, the eldest son of Mohammad Mirza and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> princess Malek Jahan Khanom. In 1835 the four-year-old Naser al-Din was named crown prince and given the governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, with regents appointed to act for him. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-shah-qajar">Mohammad Shah</a> died on 4 September 1848, he was in Tabriz, and he reached the throne with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-kabir">Amir Kabir</a>. His mother, called Mahd-e Olya, became the most powerful figure at court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The state he inherited had little power. It lacked a standing army, and at his accession the army numbered barely 3,000 men, fewer than those of various tribal leaders. Local groups kept their own militias, and many people followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwas</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a> rather than state law. In August 1852 followers of the Bab tried to assassinate the shah, and thousands of Babis were killed afterwards. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-kabir">Amir Kabir</a> was demoted and then executed on the shah's order after a court coalition formed against him.</p><p>In 1856 Naser al-Din launched the Second Herat War, and his uncle Hesam o-Saltaneh captured Herat in October after a nine-month siege. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> declared war, landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bushehr">Bushehr</a> in December, and under the Treaty of Paris of 4 March 1857 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> withdrew from Herat and renounced its claims.</p><p>He visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1873, 1878 and 1889, the first modern Iranian monarch to do so, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> made him a Knight of the Garter. He allowed newspapers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> and postal services, and patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/photography">photography</a>. A school of higher education, the Dar al-Fonun, opened in his reign, though enrolment was small. He also raised money by tax farming, and abuses by tax collectors fed resentment of a government seen as corrupt. In later years he granted concessions to Europeans for large payments. A concession of 1890 on all tobacco brought a national boycott and was withdrawn; many authorities see this as the origin of modern Iranian nationalism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-reza-kermani">Mirza Reza Kermani</a>, a follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani">Jamal al-Din al-Afghani</a>, shot the shah on 1 May 1896 while he was praying at the Shah Abdol-Azim Shrine near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>. The revolver is said to have been old and rusty. The shah was buried at the shrine and the funeral held six months later. His assassination and Kermani's execution marked a turning point in Iranian political thought that led to the Constitutional Revolution under his successor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozaffar-ad-din-shah-qajar">Mozaffar ad-Din Shah</a>. His own diary of the 1873 journey was published in several languages.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Naser al-Din Shah Qajar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Naser al-Din Shah</b> was an enthusiastic photographer who took and developed pictures himself, and thousands of Qajar-era photographs survive in the Golestan Palace collections.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Qajar Iran steadily reduced Ardalan autonomy, and in 1867 <b>Naser al-Din Shah</b> replaced the last wali with a royal governor, ending the principality.</blockquote><a href="/state/ardalan">Ardalan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kahnamu, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.86,46.19&amp;year=1873" aria-label="Kahnamu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Naser al-Din Shah Qajar"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4077 4274h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4077 4274h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.86,46.19&amp;year=1873">What was here in 1873? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
